---
title: Runtime and Scene
description: Compile object and responsive chart definitions into renderer-neutral scenes.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts separates semantic chart construction from rendering:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a definition produces a "},{"type":"link","href":"./chart-spec.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"chart spec"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"marks materialize channels"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"scales and guide layout resolve against the current size"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"marks emit a keyed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScene"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the selected SVG, Canvas, or custom renderer consumes that scene"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartRuntime"},{"type":"text","value":" for repeated renders of object or responsive\ndefinitions.\nUse "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartScene"},{"type":"text","value":" for one static compilation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"createchartruntime","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartRuntime"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createChartRuntime } from '@tanstack/charts/runtime'\n\nconst runtime = createChartRuntime<Row, Date, number>({\n  defaultTheme: platformTheme,\n})\nconst scene = runtime.render(definition, {\n  width: 800,\n  height: 400,\n})\n\nruntime.destroy()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"function createChartRuntime<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n>(options?: ChartRuntimeOptions): ChartRuntime<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartRuntime.render"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts a definition, size, and optional layout:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface RuntimeRenderSignature {\n  render(\n    definition,\n    size: { width: number; height: number },\n    layout?: {\n      measureText?: ChartTextMeasurer\n      typography?: ChartTextTypography\n    },\n  ): ChartScene\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because a standalone runtime is created before its first "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"render"},{"type":"text","value":" call, direct\nuse can supply datum, x-value, and y-value generics at the factory. DOM and\nframework adapter hosts infer them from the definition."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"runtime-behavior","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime behavior"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The runtime does not cache application data. A responsive builder receives the\ncurrent surface size and the runtime's platform "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultTheme"},{"type":"text","value":" on every direct\nrender. The same default theme is applied before the authored definition theme\nduring final scene compilation. Framework adapters and application code own\ndefinition memoization and asynchronous cleanup."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"createchartscene","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartScene"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createChartScene } from '@tanstack/charts/scene'\n\nconst scene = createChartScene(\n  staticDefinition,\n  { width: 800, height: 400 },\n  { measureText, typography },\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"function createChartScene<\n  TDatum,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue,\n>(\n  definition: StaticChartDefinition<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  size: ChartSize,\n  layout?: ChartLayoutOptions,\n): ChartScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartScene"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts static definitions only. It normalizes each scene\ndimension to at least "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":", using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":" for a nonfinite value, resolves the theme,\ninitializes every mark, collects x, y, and color channels, resolves configured\nscales, measures guides and legends, renders each mark once into the final\nchart bounds, and returns a scene value."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It throws when a materialized positional channel has no scale. Omit dimensions\nthat no mark uses; explicit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" is accepted only for an unused dimension."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"defaultcharttheme","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultChartTheme"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defaultChartTheme } from '@tanstack/charts/scene'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The exported theme contains:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"foreground: 'currentColor'"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"muted: 'currentColor'"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"grid: 'currentColor'"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"background: 'transparent'"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"six CSS-variable-backed palette entries"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Definitions merge partial overrides into this value. A supplied palette\nreplaces the default palette. An omitted focus ring defaults to enabled. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./chart-spec.md#theme","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chart spec"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"findnearestpoint","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findNearestPoint"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { findNearestPoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scene'\n\nconst point = findNearestPoint(scene, x, y, 48)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"function findNearestPoint<\n  TDatum,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue,\n>(\n  scene: ChartScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  x: number,\n  y: number,\n  maxDistance?: number,\n  points?: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n): ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue> | null","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Coordinates are in scene pixels. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxDistance"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Infinity"},{"type":"text","value":".\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"points"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.points"},{"type":"text","value":". Supplying a candidate list restricts both\nprimitive-attached and anchor-only resolution to those exact point objects.\nThe function caches a paint-ordered interaction target list for the scene,\nchecks primitive containment first, and then applies each target's natural\naxis or geometric affinity. Scene traversal accumulates group translation and\nclipping, so the resolver and renderer consume the same post-layout tree.\nSemantic points not attached to primitives retain legacy anchor distance. For\na large point set, supply a spatial index to the DOM or framework host; see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./focus-and-interaction.md#spatial-indexes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus and interaction"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"viewportinteractionpoints","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"viewportInteractionPoints"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  findNearestPoint,\n  viewportInteractionPoints,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/scene'\n\nconst candidates = viewportInteractionPoints(scene, presentationPoints)\nconst point = findNearestPoint(scene, x, y, 48, candidates)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"function viewportInteractionPoints<\n  TDatum,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue,\n>(\n  scene: ChartScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  points?: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n): readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"points"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.points"},{"type":"text","value":". With no active axis viewport, the helper\nreturns that list unchanged. Otherwise it removes off-window points only from\nmarks painted inside a viewport clip. Points from marks with fixed viewport\nownership remain available outside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.chart"},{"type":"text","value":". Pass a renderer's current\npresentation points during motion so visibility and nearest-point resolution\nuse the same painted coordinates."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"chartscene","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScene"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartScene<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  width: number\n  height: number\n  margin: ChartMargin\n  chart: ChartBounds\n  nodes: readonly SceneNode[]\n  points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n  scales: Readonly<Record<string, ResolvedScale>>\n  colors: ResolvedColorScale\n  gradients: readonly ChartGradient[]\n  theme: ChartTheme\n  focusGuides?: readonly SceneFocusGuide[]\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Property"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"width"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"height"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full scene dimensions"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"margin"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolved outer margins after guide and legend measurement"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chart"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inner plot bounds with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"width"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"height"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nodes"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ordered renderer-neutral display tree"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"points"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Complete interaction set, including viewport-clipped points"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scales"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolved positional scales, normally under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"colors"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resolved chart color scale"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gradients"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Declared linear and radial gradient resources"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"theme"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fully resolved theme"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusGuides"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Optional data-less guide descriptors resolved from current focus"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusGuides"},{"type":"text","value":" do not add nodes or points to the base scene. Every guide carries\na required resolver that receives its local focus, pointer, and cursor context\nand returns one transient scene node or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":". Surfaces pass the current\ninteraction state to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveFocusPresentation"},{"type":"text","value":", which calls those resolvers\nand separates their nodes into underlays and overlays. A mark emits\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MarkFocusGuide"},{"type":"text","value":", whose optional placement defaults from mark order. The scene\ncompiler produces the final "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneFocusGuide"},{"type":"text","value":" values shown here with placement\nresolved; renderers do not call guide resolvers or infer placement themselves."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"scene-nodes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scene nodes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every scene node has a stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":", optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"className"},{"type":"text","value":", optional\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneStyle"},{"type":"text","value":", and optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ariaHidden"},{"type":"text","value":". Geometric primitives may also attach\na "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneInteraction"},{"type":"text","value":"; groups and labels cannot."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"kind"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Geometry"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"children"},{"type":"text","value":", optional translation and clip bounds"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rule"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x1"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y1"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x2"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y2"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"polyline"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"point pairs and optional precomputed path data"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"area"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"closed points, structured polygons and holes, or optional path data"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dot"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"center and radius"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rect"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"origin, dimensions, and optional uniform or per-corner radii"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"label"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"origin, text, anchor, baseline, rotation, size, and weight"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneStyle"},{"type":"text","value":" supports fill, fill opacity, stroke, stroke opacity, stroke width,\noverall opacity, line cap, line join, and dash array."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneArea.polygons"},{"type":"text","value":" is authoritative when present. Each polygon contains an\nexterior ring followed by zero or more hole rings; one area can contain several\ndisconnected polygons. SVG and React Native serialize the rings with even-odd\nfill, while Canvas paints them directly without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Path2D"},{"type":"text","value":". Bounds and exact\ngeometry containment use every ring."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneRect.inset"},{"type":"text","value":" retains the resolved inset for absolute inline-state\noverrides. Its optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"insetAxis"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xy"},{"type":"text","value":"; vertical and\nhorizontal bars use only their categorical axis, while ordinary rectangles use\nboth axes. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneRect.maxThickness"},{"type":"text","value":" retains a bar's categorical size ceiling so\nan inline-state inset cannot widen its resolved geometry past that ceiling.\nRenderers consume the already-resolved rectangle geometry. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneRect.radius"},{"type":"text","value":"\nis the compatible uniform radius. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneRect.cornerRadii"},{"type":"text","value":" stores physical\ntop-left, top-right, bottom-right, and bottom-left radii. SVG and React Native\nrender selective corners as a path, while Canvas paints the same normalized\ngeometry directly. Custom renderers can import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveRectCornerRadii"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rectCornerRadiiPath"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/renderer/rect"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of\nduplicating that geometry policy."}]},{"type":"code","value":"type SceneInteraction =\n  | {\n      point: ChartPoint\n      points?: never\n      affinity?: 'x' | 'y' | 'xy' | 'geometry'\n    }\n  | {\n      point?: never\n      points: readonly ChartPoint[]\n      affinity?: 'x' | 'y' | 'xy' | 'geometry'\n    }","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Attach the exact point object returned in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MarkScene.points"},{"type":"text","value":". A continuous\nprimitive can attach several points, allowing one rendered line or area to\nchoose its nearest semantic sample without duplicating the primitive geometry."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom marks should return the smallest honest scene tree and stable keys. The\nfull initialization and render contracts are in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./custom-extensions.md#custom-marks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom extensions"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"interaction-points","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interaction points"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartPoint<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  key: string\n  markId: string\n  group: string | number | null\n  groupLabel: string\n  datum: TDatum\n  datumIndex: number\n  xValue: TXValue\n  yValue: TYValue\n  x1Value?: ChartValue\n  x2Value?: ChartValue\n  y1Value?: ChartValue\n  y2Value?: ChartValue\n  xInterval?: 'range' | 'difference'\n  yInterval?: 'range' | 'difference'\n  x: number\n  y: number\n  color: string\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xValue"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"yValue"},{"type":"text","value":" are semantic values inferred from mark channels. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" are scene-pixel interaction coordinates. Interval marks also retain their\nendpoints and whether the presented value is a range or endpoint difference.\nThe interaction coordinate need not describe every piece of a mark's geometry:\nan interval rect may focus at its center, a link at its midpoint, and an arrow\nat its endpoint."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"createchartruntime","text":"createChartRuntime","level":2},{"id":"runtime-behavior","text":"Runtime behavior","level":3},{"id":"createchartscene","text":"createChartScene","level":2},{"id":"defaultcharttheme","text":"defaultChartTheme","level":2},{"id":"findnearestpoint","text":"findNearestPoint","level":2},{"id":"viewportinteractionpoints","text":"viewportInteractionPoints","level":2},{"id":"chartscene","text":"ChartScene","level":2},{"id":"scene-nodes","text":"Scene nodes","level":2},{"id":"interaction-points","text":"Interaction points","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Charts separates semantic chart construction from rendering:\n\n1. a definition produces a [chart spec](./chart-spec.md)\n2. marks materialize channels\n3. scales and guide layout resolve against the current size\n4. marks emit a keyed `ChartScene`\n5. the selected SVG, Canvas, or custom renderer consumes that scene\n\nUse `createChartRuntime` for repeated renders of object or responsive\ndefinitions.\nUse `createChartScene` for one static compilation.\n\n## `createChartRuntime`\n\n```ts\nimport { createChartRuntime } from '@tanstack/charts/runtime'\n\nconst runtime = createChartRuntime<Row, Date, number>({\n  defaultTheme: platformTheme,\n})\nconst scene = runtime.render(definition, {\n  width: 800,\n  height: 400,\n})\n\nruntime.destroy()\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction createChartRuntime<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n>(options?: ChartRuntimeOptions): ChartRuntime<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n```\n\n`ChartRuntime.render` accepts a definition, size, and optional layout:\n\n```ts\ninterface RuntimeRenderSignature {\n  render(\n    definition,\n    size: { width: number; height: number },\n    layout?: {\n      measureText?: ChartTextMeasurer\n      typography?: ChartTextTypography\n    },\n  ): ChartScene\n}\n```\n\nBecause a standalone runtime is created before its first `render` call, direct\nuse can supply datum, x-value, and y-value generics at the factory. DOM and\nframework adapter hosts infer them from the definition.\n\n### Runtime behavior\n\nThe runtime does not cache application data. A responsive builder receives the\ncurrent surface size and the runtime's platform `defaultTheme` on every direct\nrender. The same default theme is applied before the authored definition theme\nduring final scene compilation. Framework adapters and application code own\ndefinition memoization and asynchronous cleanup.\n\n## `createChartScene`\n\n```ts\nimport { createChartScene } from '@tanstack/charts/scene'\n\nconst scene = createChartScene(\n  staticDefinition,\n  { width: 800, height: 400 },\n  { measureText, typography },\n)\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction createChartScene<\n  TDatum,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue,\n>(\n  definition: StaticChartDefinition<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  size: ChartSize,\n  layout?: ChartLayoutOptions,\n): ChartScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n```\n\n`createChartScene` accepts static definitions only. It normalizes each scene\ndimension to at least `1`, using `1` for a nonfinite value, resolves the theme,\ninitializes every mark, collects x, y, and color channels, resolves configured\nscales, measures guides and legends, renders each mark once into the final\nchart bounds, and returns a scene value.\n\nIt throws when a materialized positional channel has no scale. Omit dimensions\nthat no mark uses; explicit `null` is accepted only for an unused dimension.\n\n## `defaultChartTheme`\n\n```ts\nimport { defaultChartTheme } from '@tanstack/charts/scene'\n```\n\nThe exported theme contains:\n\n- `foreground: 'currentColor'`\n- `muted: 'currentColor'`\n- `grid: 'currentColor'`\n- `background: 'transparent'`\n- six CSS-variable-backed palette entries\n\nDefinitions merge partial overrides into this value. A supplied palette\nreplaces the default palette. An omitted focus ring defaults to enabled. See\n[Chart spec](./chart-spec.md#theme).\n\n## `findNearestPoint`\n\n```ts\nimport { findNearestPoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scene'\n\nconst point = findNearestPoint(scene, x, y, 48)\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction findNearestPoint<\n  TDatum,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue,\n>(\n  scene: ChartScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  x: number,\n  y: number,\n  maxDistance?: number,\n  points?: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n): ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue> | null\n```\n\nCoordinates are in scene pixels. `maxDistance` defaults to `Infinity`.\n`points` defaults to `scene.points`. Supplying a candidate list restricts both\nprimitive-attached and anchor-only resolution to those exact point objects.\nThe function caches a paint-ordered interaction target list for the scene,\nchecks primitive containment first, and then applies each target's natural\naxis or geometric affinity. Scene traversal accumulates group translation and\nclipping, so the resolver and renderer consume the same post-layout tree.\nSemantic points not attached to primitives retain legacy anchor distance. For\na large point set, supply a spatial index to the DOM or framework host; see\n[Focus and interaction](./focus-and-interaction.md#spatial-indexes).\n\n## `viewportInteractionPoints`\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  findNearestPoint,\n  viewportInteractionPoints,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/scene'\n\nconst candidates = viewportInteractionPoints(scene, presentationPoints)\nconst point = findNearestPoint(scene, x, y, 48, candidates)\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction viewportInteractionPoints<\n  TDatum,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue,\n>(\n  scene: ChartScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  points?: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n): readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n```\n\n`points` defaults to `scene.points`. With no active axis viewport, the helper\nreturns that list unchanged. Otherwise it removes off-window points only from\nmarks painted inside a viewport clip. Points from marks with fixed viewport\nownership remain available outside `scene.chart`. Pass a renderer's current\npresentation points during motion so visibility and nearest-point resolution\nuse the same painted coordinates.\n\n## `ChartScene`\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartScene<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  width: number\n  height: number\n  margin: ChartMargin\n  chart: ChartBounds\n  nodes: readonly SceneNode[]\n  points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n  scales: Readonly<Record<string, ResolvedScale>>\n  colors: ResolvedColorScale\n  gradients: readonly ChartGradient[]\n  theme: ChartTheme\n  focusGuides?: readonly SceneFocusGuide[]\n}\n```\n\n| Property          | Meaning                                                          |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `width`, `height` | Full scene dimensions                                            |\n| `margin`          | Resolved outer margins after guide and legend measurement        |\n| `chart`           | Inner plot bounds with `x`, `y`, `width`, and `height`           |\n| `nodes`           | Ordered renderer-neutral display tree                            |\n| `points`          | Complete interaction set, including viewport-clipped points      |\n| `scales`          | Resolved positional scales, normally under `x` and `y`           |\n| `colors`          | Resolved chart color scale                                       |\n| `gradients`       | Declared linear and radial gradient resources                    |\n| `theme`           | Fully resolved theme                                             |\n| `focusGuides`     | Optional data-less guide descriptors resolved from current focus |\n\n`focusGuides` do not add nodes or points to the base scene. Every guide carries\na required resolver that receives its local focus, pointer, and cursor context\nand returns one transient scene node or `undefined`. Surfaces pass the current\ninteraction state to `resolveFocusPresentation`, which calls those resolvers\nand separates their nodes into underlays and overlays. A mark emits\n`MarkFocusGuide`, whose optional placement defaults from mark order. The scene\ncompiler produces the final `SceneFocusGuide` values shown here with placement\nresolved; renderers do not call guide resolvers or infer placement themselves.\n\n## Scene nodes\n\nEvery scene node has a stable `key`, optional `className`, optional\n`SceneStyle`, and optional `ariaHidden`. Geometric primitives may also attach\na `SceneInteraction`; groups and labels cannot.\n\n| `kind`     | Geometry                                                            |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `group`    | `children`, optional translation and clip bounds                    |\n| `rule`     | `x1`, `y1`, `x2`, `y2`                                              |\n| `polyline` | point pairs and optional precomputed path data                      |\n| `area`     | closed points, structured polygons and holes, or optional path data |\n| `dot`      | center and radius                                                   |\n| `rect`     | origin, dimensions, and optional uniform or per-corner radii        |\n| `label`    | origin, text, anchor, baseline, rotation, size, and weight          |\n\n`SceneStyle` supports fill, fill opacity, stroke, stroke opacity, stroke width,\noverall opacity, line cap, line join, and dash array.\n\n`SceneArea.polygons` is authoritative when present. Each polygon contains an\nexterior ring followed by zero or more hole rings; one area can contain several\ndisconnected polygons. SVG and React Native serialize the rings with even-odd\nfill, while Canvas paints them directly without `Path2D`. Bounds and exact\ngeometry containment use every ring.\n\n`SceneRect.inset` retains the resolved inset for absolute inline-state\noverrides. Its optional `insetAxis` is `x`, `y`, or `xy`; vertical and\nhorizontal bars use only their categorical axis, while ordinary rectangles use\nboth axes. `SceneRect.maxThickness` retains a bar's categorical size ceiling so\nan inline-state inset cannot widen its resolved geometry past that ceiling.\nRenderers consume the already-resolved rectangle geometry. `SceneRect.radius`\nis the compatible uniform radius. `SceneRect.cornerRadii` stores physical\ntop-left, top-right, bottom-right, and bottom-left radii. SVG and React Native\nrender selective corners as a path, while Canvas paints the same normalized\ngeometry directly. Custom renderers can import `resolveRectCornerRadii` and\n`rectCornerRadiiPath` from `@tanstack/charts/renderer/rect` instead of\nduplicating that geometry policy.\n\n```ts\ntype SceneInteraction =\n  | {\n      point: ChartPoint\n      points?: never\n      affinity?: 'x' | 'y' | 'xy' | 'geometry'\n    }\n  | {\n      point?: never\n      points: readonly ChartPoint[]\n      affinity?: 'x' | 'y' | 'xy' | 'geometry'\n    }\n```\n\nAttach the exact point object returned in `MarkScene.points`. A continuous\nprimitive can attach several points, allowing one rendered line or area to\nchoose its nearest semantic sample without duplicating the primitive geometry.\n\nCustom marks should return the smallest honest scene tree and stable keys. The\nfull initialization and render contracts are in\n[Custom extensions](./custom-extensions.md#custom-marks).\n\n## Interaction points\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartPoint<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  key: string\n  markId: string\n  group: string | number | null\n  groupLabel: string\n  datum: TDatum\n  datumIndex: number\n  xValue: TXValue\n  yValue: TYValue\n  x1Value?: ChartValue\n  x2Value?: ChartValue\n  y1Value?: ChartValue\n  y2Value?: ChartValue\n  xInterval?: 'range' | 'difference'\n  yInterval?: 'range' | 'difference'\n  x: number\n  y: number\n  color: string\n}\n```\n\n`xValue` and `yValue` are semantic values inferred from mark channels. `x` and\n`y` are scene-pixel interaction coordinates. Interval marks also retain their\nendpoints and whether the presented value is a range or endpoint difference.\nThe interaction coordinate need not describe every piece of a mark's geometry:\nan interval rect may focus at its center, a link at its midpoint, and an arrow\nat its endpoint.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Runtime and Scene\ndescription: Compile object and responsive chart definitions into renderer-neutral scenes.\n---\n\nTanStack Charts separates semantic chart construction from rendering:\n\n1. a definition produces a [chart spec](./chart-spec.md)\n2. marks materialize channels\n3. scales and guide layout resolve against the current size\n4. marks emit a keyed `ChartScene`\n5. the selected SVG, Canvas, or custom renderer consumes that scene\n\nUse `createChartRuntime` for repeated renders of object or responsive\ndefinitions.\nUse `createChartScene` for one static compilation.\n\n## `createChartRuntime`\n\n```ts\nimport { createChartRuntime } from '@tanstack/charts/runtime'\n\nconst runtime = createChartRuntime<Row, Date, number>({\n  defaultTheme: platformTheme,\n})\nconst scene = runtime.render(definition, {\n  width: 800,\n  height: 400,\n})\n\nruntime.destroy()\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction createChartRuntime<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n>(options?: ChartRuntimeOptions): ChartRuntime<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n```\n\n`ChartRuntime.render` accepts a definition, size, and optional layout:\n\n```ts\ninterface RuntimeRenderSignature {\n  render(\n    definition,\n    size: { width: number; height: number },\n    layout?: {\n      measureText?: ChartTextMeasurer\n      typography?: ChartTextTypography\n    },\n  ): ChartScene\n}\n```\n\nBecause a standalone runtime is created before its first `render` call, direct\nuse can supply datum, x-value, and y-value generics at the factory. DOM and\nframework adapter hosts infer them from the definition.\n\n### Runtime behavior\n\nThe runtime does not cache application data. A responsive builder receives the\ncurrent surface size and the runtime's platform `defaultTheme` on every direct\nrender. The same default theme is applied before the authored definition theme\nduring final scene compilation. Framework adapters and application code own\ndefinition memoization and asynchronous cleanup.\n\n## `createChartScene`\n\n```ts\nimport { createChartScene } from '@tanstack/charts/scene'\n\nconst scene = createChartScene(\n  staticDefinition,\n  { width: 800, height: 400 },\n  { measureText, typography },\n)\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction createChartScene<\n  TDatum,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue,\n>(\n  definition: StaticChartDefinition<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  size: ChartSize,\n  layout?: ChartLayoutOptions,\n): ChartScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n```\n\n`createChartScene` accepts static definitions only. It normalizes each scene\ndimension to at least `1`, using `1` for a nonfinite value, resolves the theme,\ninitializes every mark, collects x, y, and color channels, resolves configured\nscales, measures guides and legends, renders each mark once into the final\nchart bounds, and returns a scene value.\n\nIt throws when a materialized positional channel has no scale. Omit dimensions\nthat no mark uses; explicit `null` is accepted only for an unused dimension.\n\n## `defaultChartTheme`\n\n```ts\nimport { defaultChartTheme } from '@tanstack/charts/scene'\n```\n\nThe exported theme contains:\n\n- `foreground: 'currentColor'`\n- `muted: 'currentColor'`\n- `grid: 'currentColor'`\n- `background: 'transparent'`\n- six CSS-variable-backed palette entries\n\nDefinitions merge partial overrides into this value. A supplied palette\nreplaces the default palette. An omitted focus ring defaults to enabled. See\n[Chart spec](./chart-spec.md#theme).\n\n## `findNearestPoint`\n\n```ts\nimport { findNearestPoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scene'\n\nconst point = findNearestPoint(scene, x, y, 48)\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction findNearestPoint<\n  TDatum,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue,\n>(\n  scene: ChartScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  x: number,\n  y: number,\n  maxDistance?: number,\n  points?: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n): ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue> | null\n```\n\nCoordinates are in scene pixels. `maxDistance` defaults to `Infinity`.\n`points` defaults to `scene.points`. Supplying a candidate list restricts both\nprimitive-attached and anchor-only resolution to those exact point objects.\nThe function caches a paint-ordered interaction target list for the scene,\nchecks primitive containment first, and then applies each target's natural\naxis or geometric affinity. Scene traversal accumulates group translation and\nclipping, so the resolver and renderer consume the same post-layout tree.\nSemantic points not attached to primitives retain legacy anchor distance. For\na large point set, supply a spatial index to the DOM or framework host; see\n[Focus and interaction](./focus-and-interaction.md#spatial-indexes).\n\n## `viewportInteractionPoints`\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  findNearestPoint,\n  viewportInteractionPoints,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/scene'\n\nconst candidates = viewportInteractionPoints(scene, presentationPoints)\nconst point = findNearestPoint(scene, x, y, 48, candidates)\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction viewportInteractionPoints<\n  TDatum,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue,\n>(\n  scene: ChartScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  points?: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[],\n): readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n```\n\n`points` defaults to `scene.points`. With no active axis viewport, the helper\nreturns that list unchanged. Otherwise it removes off-window points only from\nmarks painted inside a viewport clip. Points from marks with fixed viewport\nownership remain available outside `scene.chart`. Pass a renderer's current\npresentation points during motion so visibility and nearest-point resolution\nuse the same painted coordinates.\n\n## `ChartScene`\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartScene<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  width: number\n  height: number\n  margin: ChartMargin\n  chart: ChartBounds\n  nodes: readonly SceneNode[]\n  points: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n  scales: Readonly<Record<string, ResolvedScale>>\n  colors: ResolvedColorScale\n  gradients: readonly ChartGradient[]\n  theme: ChartTheme\n  focusGuides?: readonly SceneFocusGuide[]\n}\n```\n\n| Property          | Meaning                                                          |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `width`, `height` | Full scene dimensions                                            |\n| `margin`          | Resolved outer margins after guide and legend measurement        |\n| `chart`           | Inner plot bounds with `x`, `y`, `width`, and `height`           |\n| `nodes`           | Ordered renderer-neutral display tree                            |\n| `points`          | Complete interaction set, including viewport-clipped points      |\n| `scales`          | Resolved positional scales, normally under `x` and `y`           |\n| `colors`          | Resolved chart color scale                                       |\n| `gradients`       | Declared linear and radial gradient resources                    |\n| `theme`           | Fully resolved theme                                             |\n| `focusGuides`     | Optional data-less guide descriptors resolved from current focus |\n\n`focusGuides` do not add nodes or points to the base scene. Every guide carries\na required resolver that receives its local focus, pointer, and cursor context\nand returns one transient scene node or `undefined`. Surfaces pass the current\ninteraction state to `resolveFocusPresentation`, which calls those resolvers\nand separates their nodes into underlays and overlays. A mark emits\n`MarkFocusGuide`, whose optional placement defaults from mark order. The scene\ncompiler produces the final `SceneFocusGuide` values shown here with placement\nresolved; renderers do not call guide resolvers or infer placement themselves.\n\n## Scene nodes\n\nEvery scene node has a stable `key`, optional `className`, optional\n`SceneStyle`, and optional `ariaHidden`. Geometric primitives may also attach\na `SceneInteraction`; groups and labels cannot.\n\n| `kind`     | Geometry                                                            |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `group`    | `children`, optional translation and clip bounds                    |\n| `rule`     | `x1`, `y1`, `x2`, `y2`                                              |\n| `polyline` | point pairs and optional precomputed path data                      |\n| `area`     | closed points, structured polygons and holes, or optional path data |\n| `dot`      | center and radius                                                   |\n| `rect`     | origin, dimensions, and optional uniform or per-corner radii        |\n| `label`    | origin, text, anchor, baseline, rotation, size, and weight          |\n\n`SceneStyle` supports fill, fill opacity, stroke, stroke opacity, stroke width,\noverall opacity, line cap, line join, and dash array.\n\n`SceneArea.polygons` is authoritative when present. Each polygon contains an\nexterior ring followed by zero or more hole rings; one area can contain several\ndisconnected polygons. SVG and React Native serialize the rings with even-odd\nfill, while Canvas paints them directly without `Path2D`. Bounds and exact\ngeometry containment use every ring.\n\n`SceneRect.inset` retains the resolved inset for absolute inline-state\noverrides. Its optional `insetAxis` is `x`, `y`, or `xy`; vertical and\nhorizontal bars use only their categorical axis, while ordinary rectangles use\nboth axes. `SceneRect.maxThickness` retains a bar's categorical size ceiling so\nan inline-state inset cannot widen its resolved geometry past that ceiling.\nRenderers consume the already-resolved rectangle geometry. `SceneRect.radius`\nis the compatible uniform radius. `SceneRect.cornerRadii` stores physical\ntop-left, top-right, bottom-right, and bottom-left radii. SVG and React Native\nrender selective corners as a path, while Canvas paints the same normalized\ngeometry directly. Custom renderers can import `resolveRectCornerRadii` and\n`rectCornerRadiiPath` from `@tanstack/charts/renderer/rect` instead of\nduplicating that geometry policy.\n\n```ts\ntype SceneInteraction =\n  | {\n      point: ChartPoint\n      points?: never\n      affinity?: 'x' | 'y' | 'xy' | 'geometry'\n    }\n  | {\n      point?: never\n      points: readonly ChartPoint[]\n      affinity?: 'x' | 'y' | 'xy' | 'geometry'\n    }\n```\n\nAttach the exact point object returned in `MarkScene.points`. A continuous\nprimitive can attach several points, allowing one rendered line or area to\nchoose its nearest semantic sample without duplicating the primitive geometry.\n\nCustom marks should return the smallest honest scene tree and stable keys. The\nfull initialization and render contracts are in\n[Custom extensions](./custom-extensions.md#custom-marks).\n\n## Interaction points\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartPoint<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  key: string\n  markId: string\n  group: string | number | null\n  groupLabel: string\n  datum: TDatum\n  datumIndex: number\n  xValue: TXValue\n  yValue: TYValue\n  x1Value?: ChartValue\n  x2Value?: ChartValue\n  y1Value?: ChartValue\n  y2Value?: ChartValue\n  xInterval?: 'range' | 'difference'\n  yInterval?: 'range' | 'difference'\n  x: number\n  y: number\n  color: string\n}\n```\n\n`xValue` and `yValue` are semantic values inferred from mark channels. `x` and\n`y` are scene-pixel interaction coordinates. Interval marks also retain their\nendpoints and whether the presented value is a range or endpoint difference.\nThe interaction coordinate need not describe every piece of a mark's geometry:\nan interval rect may focus at its center, a link at its midpoint, and an arrow\nat its endpoint.\n"}
